  "notes": "Philadelphia's gospel-belt powerhouse and \"the lane's bridge between neo-soul harmony and radio R&B\", which is exactly what the drum block says: the entry gives two variants — \"a live-feel kit with the snare slightly late, ride-led on the ballads, tambourine on the backbeat; and a hard programmed kit with a clap on 2&4 and 16th hats for the radio singles\" — and this model holds both in one kit by layering the snare with a clap while keeping a 4–12 ms displacement, and by carrying ride, tambourine and shaker together. \"Stop-time breaks before the final chorus are a fixture\", so `fillBeforeSection` is on. The band is the stated 66–104. Harmony is gospel-cadence minor with \"diminished approach chords\" and \"extensions from a played piano, not from a pad\", hence the raised `susOrDimProb` and a seventh/ninth-weighted spread. The load-bearing claim is dynamic rather than harmonic: \"a near-whispered, conversational low verse escalating to full-belt with long melismatic runs, plus growl and cracked-voice ornament; note-density more than doubles between verse and final chorus\" — a single model cannot ramp by section, so the density range is authored wide (3–10) with `endVariation` and a raised octave jump carrying the runs, and the arrangement gives the ad-lib vamp its own long outro. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are every drum number, all melody figures, and the second progression family — the entry writes i–iv–VII–III, and the shortened i–iv is this author's reading rather than a numeral it gave. ⚠ The sub-808 the entry mentions \"on the radio-facing cuts only\" is not authored as a `bass808` block: it doubles a played bass, and authoring one would silence the bassline generator. ⚠ The half-time ballad subset is marked [LOW-CONF] in the research and is not encoded.",
